 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>CFCL tends to make clothes that get straight to the point, but things felt a little surreal upstairs in the Toguna space inside the Palais de Tokyo: chairs and a ceramic bowl seemed sunken into the concrete floor, fruit scattered between them, and a pop-inflected soundtrack by the Korean DJ duo Salamanda set a dreamy tone.</p><p>Backstage, Yusuke Takahashi explained that he wasn’t going for strange—rather, he was thinking about a changing world and how lightness can be an antidote to uncertainty. “It’s about light coming in through an atrium,” he said. “The sun streams in and reflects off the wall and gradually envelops you, so I wanted to make clothes that would feel simple, light and effortless, like you’re in harmony with nature.”</p><p>He opened with a cloudy gray handkerchief dress made of lace-motif rectangles, folded and anchored by winking, hand-sewn paillettes, an idea that carried over to a pretty white column dress and, further along, to a more transparent shape in a soft white-to-yellow gradient. The designer called this “the lucent effect,” which combines dyeing and handwork with translucent nylon and polyester-based materials.</p><p>Takahashi often draws inspiration from ceramics. This season, he revisited the idea with balloon silhouettes: one such dress hung from a graceful modernist flower choker in silver, a collaboration with the Finnish heritage jeweler Kalevala. Veil-like harem pants were another through line, paired variously with an off-the-shoulder peplum top or a voluminously layered asymmetrical bodice.</p><p>Daringness aside, those offered the kind of trend-driven statement one doesn&#x27;t usually expect from CFCL. They showed a newfound willingness to take risks, and if some of the results looked a little unwieldy or weightier than intended, it was still a welcome move. While there was plenty here to keep the CFCL base happy, this collection will likely catch the eye of a new, younger customer as wel</p></div>",
